BODY = '''<h2>What MedlinePlus and the CDC Say</h2>
<p>MedlinePlus, in its "Bathroom safety for adults" page (reviewed April 2026), advises you to sit on a bath chair or bench when taking a shower. It also recommends two grab bars, one for getting in and out of the tub and one for standing from a seated position, fixed to the wall vertically or horizontally and not diagonally, and it warns that towel racks are not grab bars because they can't support your weight.</p>
<p>For the tub floor, MedlinePlus suggests non-slip suction mats or rubber silicone decals in the tub, and a non-skid bath mat outside it. The CDC's "Check for Safety" checklist says the same about a slippery tub, suggesting a non-slip rubber mat or self-stick strips, and adds grab bars next to and inside the tub.</p>
<p>The CDC also says more than 1 in 4 older adults falls each year, that fewer than half tell their doctor, and that a single fall doubles the chance of another.</p>
<p class="note"><strong>A shower chair changes how you wash, not how you get in.</strong> You still have to step into the tub, which is why the transfer bench is the right pick for someone who cannot. If you are unsure which type you need, ask a doctor or an occupational therapist to watch you get in and out.</p>

<h2>How to Choose a Shower Chair</h2>
<h3>Can you step over the tub wall?</h3>
<p>If you can, a chair or stool inside the tub works. If you cannot do it safely, a transfer bench lets you sit outside the tub and slide across. The Drive Tub Transfer Bench here sits across the wall with two legs outside and two inside, so the tub needs a wall to sit across.</p>
<h3>Back, arms or neither</h3>
<p>A back gives you something to lean on, and arms give you something to push on. The Medline with arms and back has both. The Drive has a back, and the Medline backless and the HOMLAND have no back. More parts take up more room, so a narrow stall may suit a stool better.</p>
<h3>Weight limit</h3>
<p>The limits run from 300 to 400 pounds, and two of them come with a catch. The HOMLAND lists 400 pounds as the frame's maximum and 300 as the recommended user weight. The Drive transfer bench's page says 400 while its Amazon listing says 350. When two figures conflict, use the lower one.</p>
<h3>Seat height</h3>
<p>Listed ranges run from 14 inches on the HOMLAND to 22.5 inches on the Drive transfer bench. Choose a chair whose range lets your feet rest flat on the tub floor when you sit, and set it before you wash, not while you are wet.</p>
<h3>Feet</h3>
<p>Slip-resistant feet matter on a wet tub floor. The Drive chair's Amazon title says suction feet while its maker's page says slip reduction tips, so look at the feet on the one you receive and replace worn tips.</p>

<h2>Shower Chair Questions</h2>
<h3>Do I need a shower chair?</h3>
<p>MedlinePlus advises sitting on a bath chair or bench when you shower. It is most useful if you tire, feel unsteady or have trouble standing in a shower. A doctor or occupational therapist can tell you whether you need one and which type.</p>
<h3>Does a shower chair replace grab bars?</h3>
<p>No. MedlinePlus recommends two grab bars fixed to the wall, and says towel racks can't support your weight. A chair gives you somewhere to sit, and a grab bar gives you something to hold when you get in, out and up.</p>
<h3>What else should I do in the bathroom?</h3>
<p>MedlinePlus suggests a non-slip mat in the tub, a non-skid mat outside it, and setting the water heater to no more than 120 degrees Fahrenheit to prevent burns. It also says raising the toilet seat height can help prevent falls. See our <a href="/best-raised-toilet-seats/">raised toilet seat guide</a>.</p>
<h3>Which is better, a chair or a transfer bench?</h3>
<p>It depends on whether you can step over the tub wall. If you can, a chair is smaller and cheaper. If you cannot, the transfer bench lets you sit down outside the tub. An occupational therapist can watch you and say which fits.</p>
<h3>How tall should a shower chair be?</h3>
<p>The listed ranges are 14 to 19 inches for the HOMLAND stool, 16 to 21 for the Medline backless chair and 17.5 to 22.5 for the Drive transfer bench, and the Medline and Drive chairs adjust in 1-inch steps. Set the height so your feet rest flat on the tub floor when you sit and your knees are about level with your hips. Set it before you get wet, and check that the legs are locked.</p>
<h3>What does the weight limit really mean?</h3>
<p>The listed limits are 300 to 400 pounds, but two come with a catch. HOMLAND's listing gives 400 pounds as the frame's maximum and 300 as the recommended user weight, and Drive's page for the transfer bench says 400 where the Amazon listing says 350. When two figures conflict, use the lower one, and add the weight of anything else the chair carries.</p>

<h2>What the Research Says</h2>
<p>No trial we found tests a shower chair on its own. The Cochrane review of home safety changes found they reduced falls more for people at higher risk and when an occupational therapist delivered them, so ask for a bathroom assessment if you have already fallen. See <a href="/articles/do-home-safety-changes-prevent-falls/">the evidence summary</a>.</p>

<h2>Our Take</h2>
<p>The Medline Shower Chair with Padded Armrests and Back is the best pick for most people who can step into a tub. It has a back, two padded arms and a 350-pound rating, and 35,627 reviews average 4.7 stars. We could not read Medline's own page, so confirm the seat height range on the box.</p>
<p>The Drive Tub Transfer Bench is the right choice if stepping over the tub wall is the problem. It costs a little more, and the sources disagree on its weight limit, 400 pounds on Drive's page against 350 on the listing, so plan on 350. The Drive chair suits someone who wants the widest seat and has no use for arms, at the cost of a 300-pound limit.</p>
<p>For a small tub, the two stools are the cheapest. The Medline backless with arms keeps the 350-pound rating and the arms. The HOMLAND is lighter and has a showerhead clip, but plan on its 300-pound recommended weight, and it comes from a third-party seller.</p>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>The Medline Shower Chair with Padded Armrests and Back is the best all-round shower chair, the Drive Tub Transfer Bench is the pick if you cannot step into the tub, and the Drive chair is the choice for a wide seat. The Medline backless and the HOMLAND suit a small shower. Whichever you buy, add two wall-mounted grab bars as MedlinePlus advises, and a non-slip mat for the tub floor. See also our guide to <a href="/best-raised-toilet-seats/">raised toilet seats</a>.</p>'''

